\section{Proof sketch for Theorem~\ref{thm:partiala}:}\label{sec:proof_partiala} 

We focus on the only case where $\apreceq$ augments $\preceq$ differs, namely, comparing a more general constraint with lower an arity to a more specific constraint with higher arity.
Without loss of generality, suppose that we have $C^2$ of arity 2 and $C^3$ of arity 3, and that $C^2 \preceq C^3$.
By the definition of arity, $C^3$ can be decomposed into two arity-2 constraints denoted as $C^2_1$ and $C^2_2$.
Since $C^3 \models C^2$, one of $C^2_1$ and $C^2_2$ is semantically equivalent to $C^2$; assume it is $C^2_1$.
By the same token, one can find another arity-2 constraint that is equivalent to $C^2_2$.
As the learner traverses downward from the lowest arity value in $L$, the equivalents of $C^2_1$ and $C^2_2$ will be encountered by the learner before $C^3$.
Thus, prioritizing the more general but more succinct $C^2$ would not lead to missing $C^3$.
In fact, when the learner starts to examine $C^3$ with, the set $\mathbf{C}^2$ of learned arity-2 constraints yields $\mathbf{C}^2 \models C^3$, making it redundant. \hfill$\qed$

\section{Proof sketch for Lemma~\ref{lem:worstcase}:}\label{sec:proof_worst} 
The distribution shift caused by DC-sampling induces a worst-case evaluation scenario. 
Sampling with DC emphasizes rare and difficult examples during training, producing a learning distribution that is biased toward edge cases. 
On the other hand, the hypothesis test evaluates the learned  $\text{Th}(\mathbf{C})$ on an unbiased random sample from the population distribution, which is less concentrated on such challenging examples.
Performing well on the test sample indicates that \textit{it generalizes not only to the typical cases in the population but also to the challenging edge cases emphasized during training}. 
Consequently, the confidence value derived from the hypothesis test reflects a \textit{conservative} lower bound, ensuring robustness to both the typical population distribution and the rare edge cases. \hfill\(\qed\)


\section{Inference Rules}\label{sec:infrules}

We implement the following ten inference rules in the Fitch system~\cite{fitch1963logical}:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=12pt,labelsep=3pt] 
    \item \textbf{Assumption}: If \( \phi \) is a formula, then assume \( \phi \).
    % \[ \text{Assumption: } \quad \phi \]

    \item \textbf{Repetition}: If \( \phi \) has been derived, then \( \phi \) can be repeated.
    % \[ \text{Repetition: } \quad \phi \]

    \item \textbf{Modus Ponens}: From \( \phi \) and \( \phi \implies \psi \), infer \( \psi \):
    \[ \cfrac{\phi, \phi \implies \psi}{\psi} \]

    \item \textbf{Conjunction Introduction}: From \( \phi \) and \( \psi \), infer \( \phi \land \psi \):
    \[ \quad \cfrac{\phi, \psi}{\phi \land \psi} \]

    \item \textbf{Conjunction Elimination}: From \( \phi \land \psi \), infer \( \phi \) or \( \psi \):
    \[ \quad \cfrac{\phi \land \psi}{\phi} \quad \text{or} \quad \cfrac{\phi \land \psi}{\psi} \]

    \item \textbf{Disjunction Introduction}: From \( \phi \), infer \( \phi \lor \psi \):
    \[ \quad \cfrac{\phi}{\phi \lor \psi} \]

    \item \textbf{Disjunction Elimination}: From \( \phi \lor \psi \), \( \phi \implies \chi \), and \( \psi \implies \chi \), infer \( \chi \):
    \[ \cfrac{\phi \lor \psi, \phi \implies \chi, \psi \implies \chi}{\chi} \]

    \item \textbf{Negation Introduction}: From assuming \( \phi \) and deriving a contradiction, infer \( \neg \phi \):
    \[ \cfrac{\phi \vdash \bot}{\neg \phi} \]

    \item \textbf{Negation Elimination}: From \( \neg \neg \phi \), infer \( \phi \):
    \[ \cfrac{\neg \neg \phi}{\phi} \]

    \item \textbf{Conditional Proof}: From assuming \( \phi \) and deriving \( \psi \), infer \( \phi \implies \psi \):
    \[ \cfrac{\phi \vdash \psi}{\phi \implies \psi} \]
\end{enumerate}

