\section{GeoExplain}
\label{sec:GeoExplain}
To craft a dataset specifically for GeoGuess Task is evitable due to lack of similar training corpus. To this end, we collected a brand new dataset, GeoExplain, which consists of 5459 panorama-geoordination-explanation tuples, represented by Equation \ref{equ:GeoExplain-example}.

\begin{equation}
    G=\{(\mathbf{P_{i}},L_{i},E_{i})\}_{i=1}^{n}
    \label{equ:GeoExplain-example}
\end{equation}

Each tuple corresponds to a specific geographic location and $n$ is the total number of samples. The panorama $\mathbf{P_{i}}=\{I_{1}, I_{2}, \dots, I_{im}\}$ is a series of street view images of the location from different angles, $L_{i}$ is the exact location on street level, and the explanation $E_{i}$ provides the expert-level natural-language description how human can infer the location from the visual information in $\mathbf{P_{i}}$. Figure \ref{fig:GeoExplain Example} demonstrates an example of GeoExplain. We collected GeoExplain from an image geo-localization game GeoGuessr and its community on Reddit \cite{Geoguessrreddit}. 

We also collected a knowledge base, GeoKnowledge, which consists of geographical knowledge to assist reasoning. GeoKnowledge contains image-snippets pairs indexed by country where the snippets tell how to identify a country from visual clues in the image. GeoKnowledge can be represented by Equation \ref{equ:GeoKnowledge-example}, where $KI_{j}$ is an image of country-specific object and $S_{j}$ is the corresponding knowledge snippets.

\begin{equation}
    K=\{(KI_{j}, S_{j})\}_{j=1}^{k}
    \label{equ:GeoKnowledge-example}
\end{equation}

In this section, we firstly introduce the data collection method and then present an analysis of our dataset. To our best knowledge, GeoExplain is the first dataset which includes complete textual explanations how a location can be inferred from a panorama on expert level. 
% introduction
\subsection{Data Collection}

GeoExplain is collected from an online geographical game GeoGuessr and its community on Reddit. GeoGuessr challenges players to guess locations around the world based on Google Street View images. The game offers daily challenge mode where players are given 5 locations to guess. Thanks to its large player base and active community on Reddit, players who accurately infer these locations share their expert-level reasoning process in the daily challenge discussion threads on Reddit. We firstly selected 6085 locations from 1217 historical daily challenge from June 2021 to Sep 2024. We manually filtered these locations by following steps. 
\begin{itemize}
    \item 1) We firstly remove locations that are close to landmarks or have clearly visible landmarks, e.g., Eiffel Tower in Paris or Opera House in Sydney. 
    \item 2) We then filtered out locations where no reasonable and well-supported explanations of how to find the place were posted in the corresponding discussion threads. 
    \item 3) We also filtered out high-frequency places and ensure they are distributed across different continents, landscapes and both urban and rural areas.
\end{itemize}
After carefully filtering locations, we collected street view images in different angles from Google Street Views \cite{googlestreetview}. On the other hand, we extracted corresponding expert-level reasoning explanations from Reddit discussions. Specifically, posters usually include their scores in the discussion, so we first splited the game scores and reasoning processes. Then, we filtered these explanations as follows: 1) Remove all explanations with scores lower than 4000 (with max 5000), 2) Remove all explanations that are not in English, 3) Remove all explanations that rely on non-reasoning factors to arrive at the answer, such as living nearby or having previously finished the same challenge.

We collected GeoKnowledge also from a geographical knowledge website which is established specially for sloving the image-geolocalization puzzles. 

