\section{\label{sec:level1}Detection of trapped ions} 

\hspace{-0.35 cm}For detecting trapped ions, the RF on the cylindrical electrodes is switched off at zero phase, and simultaneously a negative high voltage (HV) pulse is applied to the grid electrode to pull the ions towards the MCP (Fig. 1(a) in the main text). Pulsed HV is required %instead of a constant DC voltage is needed because the HV should not be kept ON 
to prevent the ions that have crossed the grid from being pulled back towards the grid. Therefore the duration ($t_{HV}$) and height ($V_{HV}$) of the pulse need to be adjusted accordingly. Additionally, the pulse duration and height can be adjusted so that ions of a certain charge to mass (Q/M) ratio reach the MCP but others do not, as the grid voltage pulls them back. We have used this for differentiating between the ions generated by the LED and the 813~nm laser in Section \ref{sec:levelDistinction} below and also in probing ion resonances using parametric excitation as described in Section \ref{sec:level3} below.

