\begin{abstract}

Additional hopping channels in a tight-binding lattice is known to introduce faster dynamics of a quantum mechanical particle. However, we show that in the case of a repulsively bound state, the dynamics becomes abnormally slow when next-nearest neighbor (NNN) hopping is allowed for the particles. We show that such slowing down occurs for some magic strength of the NNN hopping at which the bound state band exhibits a quasi-flatband feature. We reveal this anomalous dynamical behavior by analyzing the quench dynamics of two nearest neighbor (NN) spin excitations (magnons) on a ferromagnetic chain by allowing both NN and NNN couplings. By implementing digital quantum computing simulations on a NISQ device, we obtain such non-trivial signatures and complement the results with exact numerical calculations. Moreover, through perturbative arguments, we reveal that the slowing down is due to the destructive interference between different paths associated to the bound state dynamics.
%\biswajit{Moreover, through perturbative arguments, we support our results, and numerically reveal that the slowing down of the bound state dynamics is due to an onset of a quasi-flatband dispersion of the bound state band, promoting destructive interference in the bound state dynamics.}

% Additionally, we also reveal that a complete edge localization of the bound state by startingcompletely localize at the e localization of the bound state due to the next-nearest-neighbor coupling.

% and nearest n, we show that the initial fast dynamics drastically slows down by switching on the next-nearest neighbour coupling  and then becomes fast with increase in the next-nearest neighbour coupling initiA bound state of two particles is The dispersion of magnon-bound states depends on the strength of anisotropy in the Heisenberg XXZ model. In the presence of the next-nearest-neighbor XX coupling in a one-dimensional system, we show that the dispersion of these bound states significantly alters. We study the system by evolving the spin excitations in the background of ferromagnetic state in a quantum computer. A critical slowdown of the bound state spreading is observed for some finite value of the next-nearest-neighbor coupling. A change from the critical coupling increases the spreading of the bound state, leading to a re-entrant behavior in the velocity of the spreading. We analytically provide the underlying physics, which shows that the next-nearest-neighbor coupling nullifies the leading order process for bound state propagation in a XXZ model. Additionally, the study reveals the edge localization of the bound state due to the next-nearest-neighbor coupling.

\end{abstract}



