	k(T) = 134 - 0.1047 T + 3.719 * 10^{-5} T^2,
\end{equation}
where the thermal conductivity $k$ is in $W/mK$ and temperature $T$ is in $^\circ$C. The visualisation of the thermal conductivity dependance on temperature, atrificial experiment setup and resulting dataset are shown in \autoref{sec:artificial_experiment}. Varying the temperature of the inside and outside boundary, $T_{\textrm{in}}$ and $T_{\textrm{out}}$, the temperature gradient and heat flux are computed using the standard finite element method and the values are saved in the material dataset for each integration point in the domain, see \autoref{fig:artificial_datasets}(b). 
