The key difference between the stronger and weaker formulations lies in the function spaces used for approximation of the unknown functions, see \autoref{tab:spaces_compared}. While the stronger formulation utilises the Sobolev space $H^1(\Omega)$ for the temperature and the Lebesgue space $\mathbf L^2(\Omega)$ for the flux, the weaker formulation uses the Lebesgue space $L^2(\Omega)$ for the temperature and the Sobolev space $H(\text{div};\Omega)$ for the flux. The weaker formulation is more natural for the heat transfer problem as it enforces continuity of the normal flux component across inner boundaries. Moreover, in the weaker formulation, the Dirichlet and Neumann boundary conditions are enforced as natural and essential, respectively, which the opposite of the stronger formulation and the standard FEM, see \autoref{fig:potato_discretised_combined}. 

Further comparisons between the stronger and weaker DD formulations are given after the derivations of the two formulations in the following subsections.

