\subsubsection{Error estimator}

The complete error estimator~\citep{braess1996posteriori} is a combination of the error indicators associated with temperature gradient~\eqref{eq:error_indicator_2}, flux divergence~\eqref{eq:error_indicator_div}, and jumps across the inner boundaries~\eqref{eq:error_indicator_jump_sum}:
\begin{equation}
    \label{eq:error_estimator}
    \begin{aligned}
        \mu_e = \left(\eta_e^2 + \nu_e^2 + \gamma_e^2 \right)^{1/2}.
    \end{aligned}
\end{equation}
The global error estimator can be computed as:
\begin{equation}
    \label{eq:error_estimator_global}
    \begin{aligned}
        \mu_g = \left( \sum_{e \in \Omega} \left[ \eta_e^2 + \nu_e^2 \right] + \sum_{l \in \Gamma} \gamma_l^2 \right)^{1/2},
    \end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $\Gamma$ refers to all inner boundaries.

The error estimator~\eqref{eq:error_estimator} can also be extended to calculate the lower and upper bounds of the numerical error \citep{braess1996posteriori}. The resulting error estimator and total error w.r.t. analytical solution
\begin{equation}
    \label{eq:total_error}
       e_\text{tot} = || T - \tilde T ||_{1, \Omega_e} + || \mathbf q - \tilde{\mathbf q} ||_{0, \Omega_e},
\end{equation}
which is the sum of the $H^1$-norm of the temperature error and $L^2$-norm of the flux error:, are shown in \autoref{fig:c3_err_combined}. 

% The estimation of upper and lower bounds of the numerical error for the weaker data-driven formulation is not in the scope of this paper, however, the error estimator can be used to identify elements where the solution is not well approximated and where the refinement is likely to improve the numerical solution accuracy.

