\subsection{Hybrid Parallelization Strategy}
For HPC platforms, hybrid parallelization utilizes multiple interconnected nodes that communicate using the MPI library (Node-level parallelism). Each node possesses a specific number of cores and memory capable of launching hardware thread (thread-level parallelism). In the hybrid parallelization on an HPC system, particles are initially divided among nodes, with each node executing the \textit{CD} and \textit{Mover} modules for its assigned particles independently. Since particle-particle interaction is mediated through the grids, a common view of the grid needs to be kept, which is facilitated through MPI communications. Nodes maintain private copies of grids with partial sums of quantites. Each node utilizes thread-level parallelization to update node-level grid quantities. Global aggregation involves communication between nodes, using the \textit{MPI\_Allreduce} function on the grid quantities to optimize costly network communication and MPI Barrier to synchronize processes. The communication overhead associated with particle decomposition stems from the need to communicate the grid quantities over a network and sum them up. Load balancing is automatically handled through particle decomposition, which is done at the beginning of the simulation. Table \ref{tab: Memory complexity} provides the details of the memory complexity associated with different implementations of \textit{CD}. 


