\section{Conclusion}
%In this article, we provide an in-depth discussion of the parallelization techniques commonly employed in conventional PIC simulations. This includes a detailed review of the primary data structures and the specific implementation of the conventional particle-decomposition-based Hybrid (OpenMP + MPI) parallelization technique for the ES-PIC. Our in-house 2D-3v PIC code, designed on conventional approach, encounters significant scalability challenges when executed on distributed HPC platforms with thousands of cores and also on shared memory-based workstations with fewer than 100 cores.
%A critical bottleneck hindering the scalability of conventional ES-PIC code is the \textit{CD} module. We propose a novel PTB based approach to address the scalability issues inherent in a conventional PIC code. We first validate the accuracy of this new approach and subsequently assess its scalability on both shared memory systems and a state of the art distributed HPC platforms. Our results indicate significant performance improvements when comparing the end-to-end runtimes of conventional and PTB based PIC methods, particularly as the core count increases. We also provide an explanation for the observed results and highlight the very little dependence of the proposed PTB based method on hardware. The proposed PTB based approach can be easily implemented while maintaining conventional data structures and requiring minimal changes to existing PIC codes.
%Additionally, we also present a benchmark of our in-house developed 2D-3v massively parallel particle-in-cell (PIC) code against a recently published benchmarking study focused on \textit{\textbf{E $\times$ B}} discharge simulation. Specifically, in a 2D axial-azimuthal simulation configuration, we demonstrate a strong quantitative agreement between our results and those of the corresponding reference by the comparative analysis of the electron energy distribution and the ion density plots with those published in prior studies \cite{charoy20192d,boeuf2018b}.
Scalability is a challenge in massively parallel Particle-in-Cell (PIC) codes when executed on large-scale distributed HPC platforms and shared-memory workstations. This limitation primarily stems from the conventional parallel private-grid-based charge deposition (CD) scheme, which scales poorly with core count as it requires a separate private grid for each thread, resulting in significant memory overhead and increased computational costs. To address this, we introduce a novel Particle Thread Binding (PTB) scheme, which significantly reduces the memory overhead by requiring only four private copies for CD in shared-memory systems and four per node in distributed-memory systems. To provide a comprehensive understanding, we first describe our in-house developed Hybrid (OpenMP + MPI) 2D-3v massively parallel PIC code, which follows the conventional private grid-based PIC approach and details its implementation and associated performance bottlenecks. Subsequently, we describe the PTB-based PIC implementation. We also validate the numerical accuracy of the PIC implementations by benchmarking it against a recently published study focused on benchmarking PIC codes for low-temperature plasmas in a \textit{\textbf{E $\times$ B}} discharge\cite{charoy20192d}. We demonstrate a strong quantitative agreement between our results and those of the benchmark simulations by the comparative analysis of the electron energy distribution and the ion density plots with those published in prior studies. Several case studies with different grid sizes and particles per cell demonstrate that the proposed PTB-based PIC significantly improves performance over conventional PIC by effectively mitigating the scalability issue associated with the conventional CD. We observed an end-to-end speedup of 2.8-12X for shared memory systems and 5-13X for distributed memory HPC systems when using PTB-PIC, compared to conventional PIC. We anticipate that this efficient PTB-based parallelization of PIC codes will significantly reduce runtimes for these resource-intensive low-temperature plasma simulations.




