\subsection{Conclusion}
Overall, the results demonstrate that the proposed sound-tracking system is capable of estimating the direction of a sound source with high accuracy and consistency. The system remains robust under different angular positions and moderate environmental noise, making it suitable for applications such as smart home automation and security surveillance.



\section{Future Work}

Based on the method presented in this study, there are several potential routes for future work to improve and expand the sound-tracking system's capabilities. One possible enhancement would be to improve the precision of the sound-tracking by including more accurate microphones and implementing a beam-forming technique in addition to our approach to compute the direction of the sound source. Furthermore, incorporating machine learning techniques could improve the system's ability to classify the direction of the sound based on audio signal characteristics, as well as anticipate the distance of the sound source.

Another direction for future work is to expand the capabilities of the system to track multiple sounds simultaneously by using multiple servo motors, each controlled by a separate set of microphones and vectors. Developing a user interface to display the direction of the sound and control the servo motor in real time could also be a valuable addition to the system.

To increase robustness, future versions of the system could integrate noise reduction methods and microphone calibration to mitigate environmental interference. The low-cost and low-complexity design has proven effective in controlled tests, and extending this to more complex environments—such as those with physical or acoustic obstacles—could help assess the system's real-world performance and limitations.

Testing the system in various environments and scenarios may provide useful insights into its performance and highlight opportunities for improvement. Exploring the method's possible applications in noise-canceling devices or security systems could also open up new avenues for this technology.


\section{Conclusion}

This paper presents a simple and cost-effective method for sound-tracking using an Arduino, three electret microphones, three LM358 op-amps, and a servo motor. By calculating the sum of vectors in rectangular form, we were able to determine the direction of the sound source with high accuracy. The results of our experiment demonstrated that the servo motor was able to track the direction of the sound source, and the recorded audio was clear and reliable.

The technique we presented in this study offers a practical and affordable solution for sound localization and tracking, with potential applications in various areas such as security, surveillance, and audio recording. It is particularly useful in scenarios where directional audio is required, such as in video conferencing, sound engineering, and audio equipment testing.

Direction detection plays a critical role in sound source localization, as it allows systems to spatially orient themselves toward a sound-emitting object. This directional awareness enhances the system's ability to respond to audio stimuli, improves context awareness in robotics and automation, and enables more efficient resource focus in smart environments. Accurately detecting the direction from which a sound originates is often the first step in identifying, isolating, or responding to the source—making it a cornerstone for real-world applications.

Moreover, this method provides an excellent alternative to expensive commercial sound-tracking systems, which are often complex and difficult to use. It offers a simple and effective way to detect and record audio and control a servo motor based on the direction of the sound.

Finally, our study provides a valuable contribution to the field of sound-tracking and offers a promising solution for sound localization and tracking with an affordable and practical approach. Future research could focus on improving the design and implementation of the sound-tracking system by incorporating additional microphones and sensors to enhance its accuracy and precision. Furthermore, additional testing and experimentation could be conducted to evaluate the system's performance in different environments and conditions.


