In connection with the recent gravitational wave event GW190814 ($2.5-2.67\,M_\odot$), modeling of compact objects becomes highly relevant for understanding the internal structure via the equation of state (EOS), which provides the interior matter distribution in a unique manner. From this perspective, in the present study, we employ a generalized polytropic EOS as proposed by \cite{Harko:2002prx}, where a modified energy density profile has been adopted alongside the Tolman-Oppenheimer-Volkoff (TOV) equation. To this end, we investigate the relationship between the masses and radii of pulsars in both general relativity (GR) and the extended framework of the modified geometric deformation (MGD) method. We have estimated the radii of four notable massive stellar objects---PSR J1614–2230 ($1.97^{+0.04}_{-0.04}\,M_\odot$), PSR J0952–0607 ($2.35^{+0.17}_{-0.17}\,M_\odot$), GW190814 ($2.5-2.67\,M_\odot$), and GW200210 ($2.83^{+0.47}_{-0.42}\,M_\odot$)---via mass-radius ($M-R$) curves that align with their observed masses. It is observed that, beyond identifying the maximum mass limits indicated by these curves, our findings lead to an intriguing discussion regarding the possible nature of matter under extreme conditions. Moreover, by analyzing the effect on the $M-R$ relationships, we can adjust the model parameters with greater flexibility in how the EOS behaves at different densities. The obtained results are physically viable, satisfying the necessary criteria for smoothness and subluminality in relativistic decoupled neutron stars.\\\\

