% we may write
%$$
%y^{\delta}_{it} = \mu^{\delta}_{jt} + \rho^{\delta}_j y^{\delta}_{it-1} + \tilde\eta^{\delta}_{it}
%$$
%where $y^{\delta}_{it}:= \log\left(\frac{V_{it}}{L_{it}}\right)$, $\mu^{\delta}_{jt}:=\frac{\rho_j\varsigma_j}{1-\varsigma_j}A_{it-1} - \frac{\varsigma_j}{1-\varsigma_j}A_{it}$ with $A_{it} \equiv \log\left(\frac{P_{L,t}}{P_{V,t}} \cdot \frac{\alpha_{V,jt}}{\alpha_{L,jt}}\right)$, and $\tilde \eta^{\delta}_{it}:= \frac{\eta^{\delta}_{it}}{1-\varsigma_j}$. Therefore, by specifying the density function of $y^{\delta}_1$ and $\tilde\eta^{\delta}_{it}$, the conditional density of $\{y^{\delta}_t\}_{t=1}^T$ can be written in the form (\ref{eq:fm}) with component-specific density function (\ref{eq:f1-dynamic}) or (\ref{eq:f1-dynamic-mixture}).
