In Figure \ref{fig:parameter-normal}, under the normality assumption, the two latent types differ significantly in their estimated mixing proportions, means, and variances, whereas no statistically significant differences are found in the estimated coefficients for $\log K_{it}$ and import shares. Specifically, compared to the first type, the second type exhibits higher mixing proportions, higher means, and lower variances consistently across all three industries. The absence of statistical evidence for differences in $\beta_{\log K}$ and $\beta_{\text{import}}$ suggests that the Cobb-Douglas specification with two-component mixtures adequately captures the observed persistence in material shares.

