This theory is called nonlinear sigma model (NLSM). The nonlinearity comes from $U^\dagger U=1$. To gain some idea about the physical meaning of coefficients $\sigma_\mu$, $c_\mu$, and $\chi_\mu$, we parametrize $U$ as $U=\exp(iW)$, where $W$ is a matrix in $\mathrm{u}(N)$. Expanding the action $S$ to the 2nd order of $W$, we obtain
\begin{equation}
    S=-\int\frac{d^dr}{8\pi}\,\sum_{\mu=1}^d\qty[\sigma_\mu\Tr\qty(\partial_\mu W)^2+c_\mu\Tr^2\qty(\partial_\mu W)-\chi_\mu\Tr(\partial_\mu W)].
\end{equation}
The hermitian matrix $W$ can be expanded on the basis of $\mathrm{u}(N)$, $W=\sum_{a=0}^{N^2-1}\pi_a T_a$, where $T_0=\mathds{1}/\sqrt{N}$ and $T_a$ ($a\ne 0$) are traceless. Therefore, 
\begin{equation}
    S=\int\frac{d^dr}{8\pi}\,\sum_{\mu=1}^d\qty[(\sigma_\mu+Nc_\mu)\qty(\partial_\mu\pi_0)^2+i\sqrt{N}\chi_\mu\Tr\qty(\partial_\mu \pi^0)+\sum_{a=1}^{N^2-1}\sigma_\mu\qty(\partial_\mu \pi_a)^2].
\end{equation}
Since $Q$-matrix is related with a pair of fermionic $\psi$-field, the propagator of $\pi$ shall be related with the propagator of diffuson, $\braket{\pi \pi}^{-1}\sim \sum_{\mu} D_\mu k_\mu^2$, where $D_\mu$ is the diffusive constant along the $\mu$th-direction. Thus, one can conclude $\sigma_\mu \sim D_\mu$, which can be interpreted as the conductivity. The symmetry also allows $c_\mu$-term and $\chi_\mu$-term. The former is related with the coupling between Goldstone modes and massive modes, while the latter is purely imaginary and is a weak topological term.

