\section{Two decoupling limits for the membrane on T$^2$}
In the limit where a bosonic membrane wraps a two-torus \( \omega \) times with \( \omega \rightarrow \infty \), the effective world-volume coupling scales as \( 1/\omega \), rendering the theory asymptotically free. This behaviour has been observed in different settings: for example, in de Wit-Hoppe–Nicolai matrix regularisation where the membrane is described by an SU(\( N \)) matrix model with coupling \( g \propto T \sim 1/\omega \) \cite{deWit:1988ig}, or the stretched wrapped membrane setting in \cite{Bjornsson:2004yp,Bjornsson:2005sh}. Let us present an incarnation of this setting in the classical membrane world-volume theory.

For this, let us consider the bosonic membrane in a toy model\footnote{In the sense, that for now we do not focus on whether this is a solution to eleven-dimensional supergravity.} background
\begin{equation*}
    \mathcal{M}_\tau \times \mathcal{M}_H = (\mathbb{R}_t \times T^2) \times \mathcal{M}_H \label{eq:toymodelgeometry}
\end{equation*}
supported by a background 4-form flux with on leg in the flat 'longitudinal' space $\mathcal{M}_\tau$ and three in the 'transverse' space $\mathcal{M}_H$.

Here, we present two (ultimately equivalent) views on a decoupling limit that can be interpreted either as a large wrapping limit around the $T^2$ or a non-relativistic expansion. In particular, we aim at the following, at leading order in these limits:
\begin{itemize}
    \item decoupling limit of the (internal three-dimensional gravity of the) membrane, i.e $\gamma_{MN} \rightarrow \eta_{MN}$
    \item membrane dynamics along $\mathcal{M}_\tau$ and $\mathcal{M}_H$ decouple 
    \item membrane dynamics along $\mathcal{M}_H$ $\rightarrow$ MZW-model
\end{itemize}
In the following, we focus on the decoupling of the internal gravity and that, at leading order, the 4-form flux decouples from the $\mathcal{M}_\tau$ membrane dynamics. In section \ref{chap:MZWfromMembrane} it is shown which concrete choice of $\mathcal{M}_H$ and $F$ leads to the MZW-model dynamics \eqref{eq:MZW} and how this can be embedded into a supergravity solution.

\subsection{Post-Newtonian dynamics}
Let us propose the following expansion for the background geometry to \eqref{eq:toymodelgeometry}
\begin{align}
    \hat{\mathrm{d}s}^2 &= c^2 \eta_{MN} \mathrm{d}x^M \mathrm{d}x^N + c^{-1} H_{mn} \mathrm{d}x^m \mathrm{d}x^n \label{eq:ToyModelBackground} \\
    \hat{F} &= F = \frac{1}{3!} F_{klmN} \mathrm{d}x^k \wedge \mathrm{d}x^l \wedge \mathrm{d}x^m \wedge \mathrm{d} x^N
\end{align}
where coordinate $x^\mu = (x^M , x^m)$ are chosen that make the split into  $\mathcal{M}_\tau$ and $\mathcal{M}_H$ manifest.\footnote{Indices of tensor of expansions (without $\hat{}$ are raised and lowered by $\eta$ or $H$.} Compared to \eqref{eq:ExpansionG}, this corresponds to the choice of clock forms $\tau^M = \mathrm{d}x^M$, which in turn causes the $c^3$-contribution to $\hat{F} = \mathrm{d} \hat{C}$ from \eqref{eq:ExpansionC} to vanish.

The striven for decoupling of three-dimensional gravity on the membrane world-volume is achieved at non-trivial leading orders of the following expansion for the coordinate fields $\hat{X}^\mu (\sigma)$
\begin{align}
    \hat{X}^\mu(\sigma) &= (\hat{X}^M(\sigma),\hat{X}^m(\sigma))  \nonumber \\
    &= \left(\sigma^M + c^{-3} X^M(\sigma) , c^{-3} X^m(\sigma) \right) .\label{eq:ExpansionNRMembrane}
\end{align}
We assume that $X^M(\sigma)$ is periodic in $\sigma^M$ for $M=1,2$. The fields $X^\mu = (X^M , X^m)$ correspond to fluctuations around a static membrane in $\mathbb{R}_t \times T^2$ that is wrapped around the $T^2$. These fluctuations are suppressed by the scale $\epsilon \sim \frac{1}{c^3}$. As desired, this expansion decouples the world-volume gravity at leading orders in $\frac{1}{c^3}$. %The   can be expressed as
% \begin{equation}
%     \hat{\gamma}_{MN} = c^2 \eta_{MN} + c^{-1} 2 \partial_{(M} X_{N)} + \mathcal{O}(c^{-4}).
% \end{equation}
The inverse of induced metric $\hat{\gamma}_{MN} = \partial_M \hat{X}^\mu \partial_N \hat{X}^\nu \hat{g}_{MN}$ then has the following expansion
\begin{equation}
    \hat{\gamma}^{MN} = c^{-2} \eta^{MN} - c^{-5} 2 \partial^{(M} X^{N)} + \mathcal{O}(c^{-8}) \label{eq:ExpansionNRinverseMetric}
\end{equation}
At leading orders indices $M,N,...$ are raised with $\eta$. Moreover, this expansion ensures that the membrane couples (at leading order) only to the leading-order background fields. Moreover, the resulting theory in the decoupling limit is a three-dimensional non-relativistic (non-Lorentz invariant) theory, as is striven for in order to reproduce the MZW-model. The \textit{equations motion at leading order} are:
\begin{align}
    0&= \frac{1}{c^5} \left( -\partial^M \partial_N X^N + \mathcal{O}\left(c^{-3}\right) \right. \nonumber \\
    &{} \quad \left. + \frac{1}{c^6}\frac{1}{3!} \eta^{MN} F_{N klm} \partial_K X^k \partial_L X^l \partial_R X^m \epsilon^{KLR} \right) \label{eq:EomDecouplingNR} \\
 0 &= \frac{1}{c^5} \Big( \partial_M \partial^M X^m + ... \nonumber \\
 &{} \quad  \left. + \frac{1}{2} H^{mn} F_{klnN} \partial_K X^k \partial_L X^l \epsilon^{KLN}  + \mathcal{O}\left(c^{-3} \right)\right) \label{eq:EomDecouplingNR2} 
\end{align}
where '$...$' denotes the Christoffel symbol contribution that will not be relevant in the following. Hence, the four-form flux that mixes longitudinal and transversal degrees of freedom decouples from the longitudinal $X^M$ equations of motion.

The leading part of the longitudinal coordinates' $X^M$ dynamics comes from a non-trivial combination the next-to-leading order of the membrane coordinate \eqref{eq:ExpansionNRMembrane} and world-volume metric \eqref{eq:ExpansionNRinverseMetric} expansions. The resulting powers of $\frac{1}{c}$ in front of the four-form flux are combinations of $\frac{1}{c^3}$ factors of the fluctuations $X^\mu$ in \eqref{eq:ExpansionNRMembrane} and the effect of raising the index of $F$ with the metric \eqref{eq:ToyModelBackground}.

\subsection{A large wrapping limit}
An alternative point of view can be provided without resorting to membrane Newton-Cartan geometry and non-relativistic interpretation by considering the more general ansatz introducing scales $R$ and $\bar{R}$ for the longitudinal and transversal spaces:\footnote{The scaling like this for $F$ has to be introduced in order to be consistent with the supergravity equations. The choice taken here is not general, but the one that will appear below.}
\begin{align}
    \hat{\mathrm{d}s}^2 &= R^2 \eta_{MN} \mathrm{d}x^M \mathrm{d}x^N + \bar{R}^2 H_{mn} \mathrm{d}x^m \mathrm{d}x^n , \label{eq:ToyModelBackgroundWrapping} \\
    \hat{F} &= F = \frac{1}{3!} R^2 \bar{R} F_{klmN} \mathrm{d}x^k \wedge \mathrm{d}x^l \wedge \mathrm{d}x^m \wedge \mathrm{d} x^N .
\end{align}
In contrast to the non-relativistic setting, we look at arbitrary fluctuations around a static wrapped membrane in the large wrapping number limit $\omega \rightarrow \infty$:
\begin{align}
\hat{X}^\mu(\sigma) &= \left( \hat{X}^M(\sigma) , \hat{X}^m(\sigma) \right) \\
&= \left( \omega \sigma^M + X^M(\sigma) , X^m(\sigma) \right). \nonumber
\end{align}
The parameter $\omega$ describes both a 'world-volume speed of light' and the wrapping number around the torus.\footnote{In principle, these parameters could be different, of course -- i.e. $ X^M = ( \omega_0 \sigma^0, \omega_1 \sigma^1 , \omega_2 \sigma^2)$. For the limit in question, we want to assume $\omega_0 \sim \omega_1 \sim \omega_2 \sim \omega \rightarrow \infty$. Finite quotients could be compensated for by rescaling of the membrane coordinates $\sigma^M$.} In the limit $\omega \rightarrow \infty$, the $\mathcal{O}(\omega^0)$ world-volume fields $x^\mu(\sigma)$ are suppressed and the induced metric simplifies significantly.
% \begin{equation}
%     \gamma_{MN} = \omega^2 \left( \eta_{MN} + 2 \partial_{(M} X_{N)} \omega^{-1} + \mathcal{O}(\omega^{-2}) \right)
% \end{equation}
In particular, one can compute the inverse 
\begin{equation}
    \gamma^{MN} = \frac{1}{\omega^2 R^2} \left( \eta^{MN} - \frac{1}{\omega} 2 \partial^{(M} x^{N)} + \mathcal{O}(\omega^{-2}) \right).
\end{equation}
in order to compute the expansion of the equations of motion in leading order 
{\small \begin{align}
   0 &= -  \frac{1}{\omega^2 R^2} \left( \partial^K \partial_M X^M + \mathcal{O} \left( \frac{1}{\omega} \right) \right)  \label{eq:EomDecouplingWrapping} \\
   &{} \quad + \frac{\bar{R}^2}{R} \frac{1}{3!} \eta^{KM} F_{Mklm} \partial_K X^k \partial_L X^l \partial_R X^m \epsilon^{KLR} ,  \nonumber \\
   0 &=  \frac{1}{\omega^2 R^2} \left( \partial_M \partial^M X^m + ... \right) \label{eq:EomDecouplingWrapping2} \\
 &{} \quad  + \frac{1}{2} R \eta^{mn} F_{klnN} \partial_K X^k \partial_L X^l \epsilon^{KLN}  + \mathcal{O}\left(\frac{1}{\omega^3 R^2} \right) . \nonumber
\end{align}} 

Requiring that the four-form flux couples to the leading order transverse dynamics and decoupling of the longitudinal and transverse degrees of freedom at leading order leads to the following scaling for the (dimensionless) scales $R$ and $\bar{R}$:
\begin{equation}
    R^3 \sim \frac{1}{\omega^2} \rightarrow 0 , \qquad \mathcal{O}(\bar{R}^2) < \mathcal{O} \left( \frac{1}{\omega^2 R} \right). \label{eq:Rscaling}
\end{equation}
The results of the two decoupling limits -- the post-Newtonian and the large wrapping one -- agree for $\omega \sim c^3$, $R \sim c$, and $\bar{R} \sim \frac{1}{\sqrt{c}}$. \pagebreak

\section{The MZW-model as decoupling limit of the membrane}
\label{chap:MZWfromMembrane}

\subsection{A toy model background}
In order to obtain the MZW-model from the transverse equations of motion at leading order, like \eqref{eq:EomDecouplingNR2}, we specify $\mathcal{M}_H$ to be a Lie group $G$.\footnote{For now, we are not concerned with dimensionality of the background.} The background expansion in the conventions for the non-relativstic limit are
\begin{align}
    \hat{\mathrm{d}s}^2 &= c^2 \eta_{MN} \mathrm{d}x^M \mathrm{d}x^N + c^{-1} \kappa_{ab} e^a e^b \label{eq:ToyModelBackgroundMZW} \\
    \hat{F} &= F = - \frac{1}{3!} f_{abc} e^a \wedge e^b \wedge e^c \wedge v .  \nonumber
\end{align}
$e^a = {e_m}^a \mathrm{d}x^m$ are the Maurer-Cartan one-forms on $G$, $f_{abc}$ are the structure constants for the Lie algebra $\mathfrak{g}$ of $G$, and $\kappa$ is the Killing form on $\mathfrak{g}$. $v$ is a one form on $\mathcal{M}_\tau$. We choose $v = \mathrm{d}x^0$ or $v = \mathrm{d}x^{1/2}$. This choice will be related to the choice of $v$ in the MZW-model \eqref{eq:MZW}. In the same way as in the previous section, at leading order the membrane equations of motion, now expressed in terms of currents $j^a = {e_m}^a \mathrm{d} X^m$, are
\begin{align}
    0 &= \partial_M \partial_N X^N, \\
    0 &= \partial^N j_N^a - \frac{1}{2}{f^a}_{bc} j^{b}_M j^c_N v_L \epsilon^{MNL}.´, 
\end{align}
for the expansion \eqref{eq:ExpansionNRMembrane}. We recognise the MZW equation of motion \eqref{eq:MZW} for the transverse directions. The longitudinal equations of motion $\partial_M \partial_N X^N = 0$ decouple, but are solvable in their own right. For the purpose of this paper, we will assume the solution $X^N = 0$ in the following.

\subsection{Embedding into a supergravity solution}
The toy model background \eqref{eq:ToyModelBackgroundMZW} is not a solution to eleven-dimensional supergravity, but can be embedded in an eleven-dimensional supergravity solution. The prototypical example will be provided by the eleven-dimensional uplift of the AdS$_3 \times$S$^3 \times$T$^4$ background, due to the presence of a group manifold being supported by four-form flux:
\begin{align*}
    \mathrm{d}s^2 &= R^2 \left( \mathrm{d}s^2_{\text{AdS}_3} + \mathrm{d}s^2_{\text{S}^3} \right) + \mathrm{d}s^2_{T^4} + \mathrm{d}z^2 \\
    F &= 2R^2 \left( Vol(\text{AdS}_3) + Vol(\text{S}^3 ) \right) \wedge \mathrm{d}z
\end{align*}
where $z$ is the coordinate of the M-theory circle. Moreover, let us single out one of the directions of the $T^4$, denoted by $y$, and introduce the scales $R$ and $\bar{R}$
\begin{align}
    \mathrm{d}s^2 &= R^2 \left( \mathrm{d}s^2_{\text{AdS}_3} + \mathrm{d}s^2_{\text{S}^3} \right) + \bar{R}^2 \left( \mathrm{d}y^2 + \mathrm{d}z^2 \right) +  \mathrm{d}s^2_{T^3} \nonumber  \\
    F &= 2R^2 \bar{R} \left( Vol(\text{AdS}_3) + Vol(\text{S}^3 ) \right) \wedge \mathrm{d}z .
\end{align}
With the following expansion (in a Poincar\'e patch of AdS$_3$) for example:
\begin{align}
    \hat{X}^\mu(\sigma) &= \left( X_{AdS_3}(\sigma) ; X_{S^3}^m(\sigma) ; X_y(\sigma) , X_z(\sigma) , X_{T_3}(\sigma) \right) \nonumber \\
    X_{AdS_3}(\sigma) &= (t(\sigma),x(\sigma),r(\sigma)) = \left( \omega \frac{\bar{R}}{R} \sigma^0 , 0 , r_0 \right) \label{eq:ExpansionAdS3S3} \\
    X_y (\sigma) &= \omega \sigma^1, \quad X_z(\sigma) = \omega \sigma^2, \quad X_{T^3}(\sigma) = const. \nonumber
\end{align}
Hence, the focus lies on the transversal fluctuations $X^m_{S^3}$ around the $S^3$, neglecting the other (finally decoupled) fluctuations -- both along the remaining transversal directions but also the longitudinal ones. The only significant difference from the toy model case is that the time-like direction lies in AdS$_3$ which scales like the transverse direction. In the above expansion, this is cured by the parameterisation of $t(\sigma)$ including the ration $\frac{\bar{R}}{R}$. Hence, for a large wrapping limit $\omega \rightarrow \infty$, and suitable limits for $R$ and $\bar{R}$, as discussed above \eqref{eq:Rscaling}, one reproduces the MZW-model equations of motion for the fluctations $X_{S^{3}}^m$.

\section{Outlook}
In this letter, we proposed a construction that shows that a certain regime of the (bosonic) membrane can be described by a classically integrable theory, the $(1+2)$-dimensional MZW-model, which has been studied in great detail in the literature. In this way, this proposal also offers a physical realisation of this model. Some crucial questions for future research will be:
\begin{itemize}
    \item What is the physical interpretation of the conserved charges \cite{Ioannidou:1995he} and integrable hierarchy \cite{Dimakis:1999sw,wu2008cauchyproblemwardequation} of the MZW-model for the membrane? Do these also have meaning outside of this regime?
    \item What is the physical interpretation of the solitons \cite{Ward:1990vc,Ward:1995he,Ioannidou:1999} of the MZW-model on the membrane?
    \item Is there a dual holographic picture, corresponding to the expansion \eqref{eq:ExpansionAdS3S3} of the eleven-dimensional uplift of the NSNS AdS$_3 \times$S$_3$ background?
\end{itemize}
Moreover, for the integrable two-dimensional $\sigma$-models, generalised geometry resp. O$(d,d)$-duality, was used in order to construct and understand new integrable models \cite{Osten:2019ayq,Borsato:2021gma,Borsato:2021vfy}. For the membrane a similar formulation, geometrising an underlying $E_{d(d)}/K_d$-structure, has been obtained and discussed in the literature \cite{Hatsuda:2012vm,Sakatani:2020iad,Sakatani:2020umt,Osten:2021fil,Hatsuda:2023dwx,Osten:2024mjt}. It might be fruitful to systematically study non-relativistic limits and post-Newtonian expansions of this setting in order to obtain new regimes of membrane theory that are described by integrable $(1+2)$-dimensional field theories. In general, the advent of novel higher-dimensional integrable theories via decoupling limits could be an interesting consequence of the present work. As mentioned above, another potential path to new higher-dimensional integrable $\sigma$-models could be possible via the recently suggested higher Chern-Simons theory \cite{Schenkel:2024dcd}. \pagebreak


